  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 8e2e2318533cdee478cc197dc2db33bc, type: 3}
  m_Name: ThrowingBall
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  displayImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 82e1a62900446b7418908e524fcdbd27, type: 3}
  displayText: If you throw an object at an angle (not 45 degrees), is the Velocity
    equal in the vertical and horizontal axis (Angle = 90 and 0)?<br>Would the velocity
    or speed, in these axis be same/different from the velocity in the thrown angle?<br>Imagine
    you are throwing a ball...
  solutionImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 82e1a62900446b7418908e524fcdbd27, type: 3}
  solutionText: 'No, if you throw a ball at an angle,<br> its speed can be broken
    up different directions and it is dependent on the angle thrown.<br>You might''ve
    imagined throwing a ball straight up or throwing a ball to a friend. <br> Fun
    fact: The farthest you can throw a ball is Angle = 45, spliting the speeds equally.'
